on into a single finding.\n\nClassify each finding as **human** or **bot**:\n- **Bot** — author is a GitHub bot, CI system, or automated tool (e.g. `github-actions[bot]`, `codecov[bot]`, linters, static analysis)\n- **Human** — everything else\n\n## 4. Present Findings\n\nPresent two separate sections:\n\n### Human Comments (addressed individually)\n\nNumbered list, ordered by:\n\n1. **Bugs / correctness issues** — reviewer identified broken logic or missing error handling\n2. **Design / architecture feedback** — structural changes, API shape, naming of public interfaces\n3. **Style / nits** — formatting, naming of local variables, minor readability\n\nFor each finding, show:\n- **#N** — `file.go:42` — One-line summary of what the reviewer is asking for\n- **Reviewer** — who asked\n- **Quote** — the key sentence from their comment (verbatim)\n\n### Bot Comments (batched)\n\nNumbered list (continuing the numbering from above), grouped by tool/bot. For each:\n- **#N** — `file.go:42` — One-line summary of the required fix\n- **Bot** — which tool flagged it\n\nEnd with a summary: total human comments, total bot comments, overall assessment of effort.\n\n**STOP and wait for user confirmation.** The user may reorder, skip, or drop items from either section.\n\n## 5. Fix Bot Comments (batched)\n\nFix all confirmed bot comments first — these are mechanical and clearing them reduces noise before the slower human-comment phase.\n\n\n1. For each bot finding:\n   - Read the relevant code\n   - Implement the fix — ONLY the changes needed for that single finding\n   - If a fix is ambiguous or would conflict with a human-comment fix already applied, STOP and ask\n2. After all bot fixes are applied, show a combined diff and a summary table:\n\n| # | Finding | File | Bot | Status |\n|---|---------|------|-----|--------|\n| 8 | Description | path:line | linter-name | Fixed |\n| 9 | Description | path:line | linter-name | Fixed |\n| 11 | Description | path:line | linter-name | Skipped — conflicts with #3 |\n\n3. **STOP and wait.** The user reviews the combined diff.\n\n## 6. Fix Human Comments (one at a time)\n\nAfter bot fixes are reviewed, work through the confirmed human comments one at a time:\n\n1. State which finding you are addressing (number and one-line description)\n2. Read the relevant code and the full comment thread to understand intent\n3. Implement the fix — ONLY the changes needed for that single finding\n4. Show the diff\n5. **STOP and wait for user approval** before proceeding to the next finding\n\n### Scope Rules\n\n- Make the MINIMAL change that addresses the reviewer's feedback\n- Do NOT rename variables, reformat code, or touch lines outside the feedback scope\n- Do NOT refactor adjacent code, even if it looks related\n- If the reviewer's comment is ambiguous, STOP and ask the user for clarification rather than guessing\n- Do NOT create any git commits — code changes only\n\n## 7. Verify Fixes\n\nAfter all fixes are applied, run the project's lint and test commands scoped to only the changed files and their directly related tests:\n\n- **Lint / static analysis** — run the project's linter scoped to the files that were modified. For example: `golangci-lint run ./path/to/...`, `eslint src/changed-file.ts`, not a full-repo lint.\n- **Tests** — run only the test files that cover the modified code (same package, same module, co-located test files). Do NOT run the full test suite.\n\nShow the COMPLETE output. Do not summarize or truncate.\n\nIf lint or tests fail due to issues introduced by the fixes:\n\n1. Read the error output and identify every failure\n2. Fix all issues — apply the minimal changes needed\n3. Re-run the failing commands\n4. Show the complete output again\n\nCap at **2 fix attempts**. If still failing after 2 rounds, present the remaining failures to the user with full output.\n\nOnce verification passes, show a combined diff of all changes and a summary: how many comments were addressed vs skipped, and remind the user to push and re-request review if ready.\n\n## 8. Post-Fix\n\nAfter the user has reviewed the combined diff:\n\n- If the user asks for adjustments, apply them and show the updated diff\n- If the user asks to revert specific fixes, revert ONLY those changes\n- Do NOT commit. The user handles commits on their own terms.\n\n## 9. Reply to PR Comments\n\nAfter the user has committed and pushed, offer to reply to the addressed review threads. **STOP and wait for user approval** before posting any replies.\n\nIf approved, for each addressed finding:\n\n- Reply in the original comment thread using `gh api` with a brief description of what was changed (e.g. \"Fixed — added nil check before dereferencing\" or \"Updated to use the existing `ParseConfig` helper\")\n- Keep replies to one sentence. Do NOT over-explain or repeat the diff.\n- Do NOT reply to skipped or dropped findings\n- After replying, resolve the thread\n"}}
