es wrong if this isn't fixed\n- **Evidence** — how the finding was confirmed (code path, call site, test gap)\n- **Suggested fix** — concrete code change, not vague advice\n\nIf no findings exist at a severity level, omit that section.\n\nIf there are 0 findings across all agents, report that the review is clean and stop. Do NOT present the fix mode menu.\n\n## 4. Present Report and Choose Fix Mode\n\nPresent findings in two sections:\n\n### Required\n\nNumbered list of findings classified as Required, sorted by severity.\n\n### Improvements (follow-up)\n\nNumbered list (continuing the numbering) of findings classified as Improvement. These are presented for awareness but are NOT included in the fix cycle by default.\n\nEnd with a one-paragraph summary: total required vs improvement findings, overall merge-readiness assessment, and any patterns across files.\n\nThen offer two fix modes — these apply to Required findings only:\n\n> **How would you like to proceed?**\n>\n> 1. **Supervised** — I fix one required finding at a time and wait for your approval before the next.\n> 2. **Batched** — I fix all required findings in the order presented and show a combined diff at the end. You can optionally specify a subset (e.g. \"fix 1,3,5-8\") or include specific improvement findings.\n>\n> You can also drop, reorder, or skip any findings.\n\n**STOP and wait for the user's choice.**\n\n## 5. Fix Cycle\n\n### Scope Rules (all modes)\n\n- Make the MINIMAL change that addresses the finding\n- Do NOT rename variables, reformat code, or touch lines outside the finding scope\n- Do NOT refactor adjacent code, even if it looks related\n- Do NOT create any git commits — code changes only\n\n### Mode: Supervised\n\nWork through the user's list one at a time:\n\n1. State which finding you are fixing (number and one-line description)\n2. Read the relevant code to confirm the fix approach\n3. Implement the fix — ONLY the code changes for that single finding\n4. **STOP and wait for user approval** before proceeding to the next finding\n\nWhen the user indicates they are done (e.g. \"that's all\", \"done\", \"stop\"), or all findings have been addressed, proceed to Step 6 (Verify Fixes).\n\n### Mode: Batched\n\nFix all Required findings in report order by default. If the user specified a subset (e.g. \"fix 1,3,5-8\"), fix only those — this allows including specific Improvement findings if desired. Do NOT ask which findings to fix — start immediately.\n\n1. For each finding:\n   - Read the relevant code to confirm the fix approach\n   - Implement the fix — ONLY the code changes for that single finding\n   - If a fix would require changing a function signature in a shared interface, or the fix approach is ambiguous, STOP and ask before proceeding\n2. After all fixes are applied, proceed directly to Step 6 (Verify Fixes). Do NOT show a diff yet.\n\n## 6. Verify Fixes\n\nRun the project's lint and test commands scoped to only the changed files and their directly related tests:\n\n- **Lint / static analysis** — run the project's linter scoped to the files that were modified by the fixes. For example: `golangci-lint run ./path/to/...`, `eslint src/changed-file.ts`, not a full-repo lint.\n- **Tests** — run only the test files that cover the modified code (same package, same module, co-located test files). Do NOT run the full test suite.\n\nShow the COMPLETE output. Do not summarize or truncate.\n\n### On Failure: Auto-Fix\n\nIf lint or tests fail due to issues introduced by the review fixes:\n\n1. Read the error output and identify every failure\n2. Fix all issues — apply the minimal changes needed\n3. Re-run the failing commands\n4. Show the complete output again\n\nCap at **2 fix attempts**. If still failing after 2 rounds, present the remaining failures to the user with full output.\n\n## 7. Present Results\n\nOnce verification passes (or the user has seen the remaining failures), present a summary table. Do NOT show a diff — the Edit tool already showed 